    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Susa was settled from about 4200 BCE, came within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a> cultural sphere, and became the centre of Elamite civilisation, which Mesopotamian rulers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-of-akkad">Sargon</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashurbanipal">Ashurbanipal</a> conquered or plundered in turn.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> took it in 540 to 539 BCE, and under Darius it became one of the Achaemenid capitals, but it lost its imperial standing after Alexander and under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucids</a>.</li><li>Parthian and Sasanian kings used it as a refuge and a trading town, an Arab army took it in 642, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> razed it in 1218, after which it never regained its importance.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In about 1175 BCE the Elamite king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shutruk-nahhunte">Shutruk-Nahhunte</a> plundered the original stele bearing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/code-of-hammurabi">Code of Hammurabi</a> and took it to Susa, where archaeologists found it in 1901.</p></aside>
    </section>

