    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 4200 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">A settlement is founded at Susa and a monumental platform is raised</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 2330 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-of-akkad">Sargon</a> the Great incorporates Susiana into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1175 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shutruk-nahhunte">Shutruk-Nahhunte</a> takes the stele of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/code-of-hammurabi">Code of Hammurabi</a> to Susa</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">647 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashurbanipal">Ashurbanipal</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> levels the city</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">540 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> takes Susa, which becomes part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">339</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shapur-ii">Shapur II</a> destroys the city after a rebellion of its Christians</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">642</span><span class="rf-text">An Arab army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-musa">Abu Musa</a> al-Ash'ari captures Susa</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1218</span><span class="rf-text">Invading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> raze the city</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Susa lay in the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagros-mountains">Zagros Mountains</a> about 250 km east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a>, between the Karkheh and Dez Rivers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. Based on calibrated carbon-14 dating, a settlement was founded there as early as 4200 BCE, which makes Susa one of the oldest known settlements of the region. The founding corresponded with the abandonment of nearby villages, and Potts suggests that the settlement may have been founded to try to re-establish the destroyed settlement at Chogha Mish, about 25 km to the west, which had a similar massive platform.</p><p>Soon after the first settlement its inhabitants erected a monumental platform that rose over the flat surrounding landscape. Two settlements that archaeologists call the Acropolis (7 ha) and the Apadana (6.3 ha) later merged to form Susa proper (18 ha), and the Apadana was enclosed by walls of rammed earth 6 metres thick. Nearly two thousand pots of Susa I style were recovered from a cemetery near the base of the platform, most of them now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a>. The painted pottery was a late, regional version of the Mesopotamian Ubaid ceramic tradition and was made carefully by hand. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">Copper</a> metallurgy is also attested: as many as 40 copper axes and 10 round discs, probably mirrors, were found in the cemetery.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Susa&#8221; cites where it covers Name, Early settlement:</p><ul><li>Walther Hinz, Elamisches Wörterbuch (Dietrich Reimer Verlag, 1987)</li><li>Daniel T. Potts, The archaeology of Elam: Formation and transformation of an ancient Iranian state (Cambridge University Press, 1999)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/darius-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/darius-i.jpg" alt="Darius I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Darius_I.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rumlu, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk-period">Uruk period</a> Susa came within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a> cultural sphere. Its remains show an imitation of the whole state apparatus of Uruk, with proto-writing, cylinder seals with Sumerian motifs and monumental architecture. Scholars dispute how far Uruk shaped the city. Holly Pittman holds that the Susanians took part entirely in an Uruk way of life, while Potts argues that Susa adopted writing and numbers only partially and selectively, and that highland influence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khuzestan">Khuzestan</a> was strong as well. From about 3100 BCE Banesh pottery predominated and the Proto-Elamite tablets first appear, and Susa then became the centre of Elamite civilisation. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> seals and etched carnelian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a> found in the city from the period 2400 to 2100 BCE point to relations with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-valley">Indus Valley</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius the Great</a> began a major building programme at Susa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persepolis">Persepolis</a> that included a large palace, and he recorded that the earth was dug down to rock and packed with rubble 40 and 20 cubits deep before the palace was built. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucids</a> the city kept its economic importance, with many merchants trading there and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charax-spasinou">Charax Spasinou</a> as its port. Under Sasanian rule the settlement extended over 400 hectares, and Susa remained a trading centre, especially for gold. It was a manufacturing centre of luxury fabrics into the Islamic period.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sargon-of-akkad"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sargon-of-akkad.jpg" alt="Sargon of Akkad" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sargon-of-akkad">Sargon of Akkad</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sargon_of_Akkad_on_his_victory_stele.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ALFGRN, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In the Sumerian period Susa was the capital of a state called Susiana, and control of Susiana shifted between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumer">Sumer</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkad">Akkad</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-of-akkad">Sargon</a> the Great incorporated it into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire</a> in about 2330 BCE. Susa was an Akkadian province until about 2100 BCE, when its governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutik-inshushinak">Kutik-Inshushinak</a> rebelled and made it independent. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-dynasty-of-ur">Third Dynasty of Ur</a> then held it until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a> fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elamites">Elamites</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kindattu">Kindattu</a> in about 2004 BCE, and Susa became the capital of Elam under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimashki-dynasty">Shimashki dynasty</a>. From about 1500 BCE the Anshanite kings took the title king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anshan">Anshan</a> and Susa. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-i">Nebuchadnezzar I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> plundered the city about fifty years after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shutruk-nahhunte">Shutruk-Nahhunte</a>'s raid. Assyrian rule lasted from 647 BCE until the Median capture of Susa in 617 BCE.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> captured Susa between 540 and 539 BCE, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambyses-ii">Cambyses II</a> it was one of four Achaemenid capitals. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrap</a> surrendered to Alexander, and under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucids</a> it lost the standing of an imperial capital and became the regional capital of the satrapy of Susiana. Around 147 BCE Susa and Elymais broke away from the Seleucid Empire, and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phraates-ii">Phraates II</a> almost all Parthian rulers coined money there. The Roman emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a> captured it briefly in 116, and it became Parthian again in 215. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardashir-i">Ardashir I</a> conquered it in 224, and an Arab army took it in 642.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Susa&#8221; cites where it covers Susa I period (4200–3800 BC), Seleucid period, Parthian period, Sassanid period:</p><ul><li>Prudence Harper, The Royal City of Susa: Ancient Near Eastern Treasures in the Louvre (The Metropolitan Museum of Art, 1992)</li><li>Daniel T. Potts, The archaeology of Elam: Formation and transformation of an ancient Iranian state (Cambridge University Press, 1999)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/chogha-zanbil"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chogha-zanbil.jpg" alt="Chogha Zanbil" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/chogha-zanbil">Chogha Zanbil</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D8%B2%DB%8C%DA%AF%D9%88%D8%B1%D8%A7%D8%AA_%DA%86%D8%BA%D8%A7_%D8%B2%D9%86%D8%A8%DB%8C%D9%84.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mobina ghanbaryan, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Susa's main goddess was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanaya">Nanaya</a>, who had a significant temple there. Under the Middle Elamite kings the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elamite-language">Elamite language</a> and pantheon were imposed in Susiana, and the political and religious complex at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chogha-zanbil">Chogha Zanbil</a>, 30 km south-east of the city, marked the height of that policy. The Seleucid city was named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucia">Seleucia</a> on the Eulaeus. It is rich in Greek inscriptions, which perhaps indicate a significant number of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, and large, well-equipped peristyle houses have been excavated in the royal city. It kept its Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-state</a> organisation well into the Parthian period.</p><p>Susa had a Christian community in a separate district with a Nestorian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>, the last of whom is attested in 1265, and a stucco panel with the image of a Christian saint has been found. After the Arab conquest a mosque was built, and a Jewish community with its own synagogue is recorded. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shapur-ii">Shapur II</a> resettled the rebuilt city with prisoners of war and weavers who made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> brocade, and he renamed it Eran-Khwarrah-Shapur. The tomb attributed to the prophet Daniel stood there, and after the conquest Muslims and Christians made pilgrimages to it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benjamin-of-tudela">Benjamin of Tudela</a> cited Susa as Daniel's burial place at least as early as 1167.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/shutruk-nahhunte"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shutruk-nahhunte.jpg" alt="Shutruk-Nahhunte" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/shutruk-nahhunte">Shutruk-Nahhunte</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shutruk-Nakhunte,_Mus%C3%A9e_du_Louvre.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Darafsh, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The city was plundered and destroyed several times. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shutruk-nahhunte">Shutruk-Nahhunte</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-i">Nebuchadnezzar I</a> took its wealth in the 12th century BCE. In 647 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashurbanipal">Ashurbanipal</a> levelled the city during a war in which its people had fought on the other side. His inscription records that he destroyed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziggurat">ziggurat</a>, smashed its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> horns, devastated the tombs of the Elamite kings and sowed salt on the lands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardashir-i">Ardashir I</a> conquered and destroyed Susa in 224, and it was rebuilt immediately. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shapur-ii">Shapur II</a> persecuted the Christians of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> from 339, and after a rebellion of Christians in Susa he destroyed the city that year using 300 elephants. He later rebuilt it, which is believed to have followed his victory over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amida">Amida</a> in 359. In 642 the Arab army besieged Susa, and Muslim sources give two stories of how it fell. After entering the city the Muslims killed all the Persian nobles. They also took the treasure stored at Daniel's tomb and removed the corpse, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> Umar ordered the signet ring returned and the body reburied under the riverbed.</p><p>The city recovered, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> razed it in 1218 and it never regained its previous importance. In the 15th century most of its population moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dezful">Dezful</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/xerxes-i.jpg" alt="Xerxes I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:National_Museum_of_Iran_Darafsh_(785).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Darafsh, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Susa appears in the earliest Sumerian records, among the places obedient to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inanna">Inanna</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enmerkar">Enmerkar</a> and the Lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aratta">Aratta</a>. As Shushan it is named in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ezra">Ezra</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nehemiah">Nehemiah</a>, Daniel and above all the Book of Esther, which is the basis of the Jewish holiday of Purim and is set in Susa. The king Ahasuerus of that book may refer to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes I</a>. Susa is also the setting of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeschylus">Aeschylus</a>'s The Persians of 472 BCE, the oldest surviving play in the history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>.</p><p>The site was examined in 1836 by Henry Rawlinson, and Marcel-Auguste Dieulafoy and Jane Dieulafoy began the first French excavations in 1885. Jacques de Morgan excavated from 1897 to 1911 and found the stele of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naram-sin">Naram-Sin</a>, Babylonian boundary stones, the stele bearing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/code-of-hammurabi">Code of Hammurabi</a> and thousands of inscribed bricks, many of which went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a>. His finds showed Susa to be the most important centre of Elamite civilisation. He and his successor Roland de Mecquenem used destructive methods that damaged much of the later historical layers. Roman Ghirshman and his wife excavated the hectare-sized Ville Royale down to bare earth from 1946 to 1967, and the pottery found at its levels gave Susa a stratigraphy.</p><p>The ancient centre is unoccupied today, and the town of Shush lies west and north of the ruins. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> inscribed Susa on the World Heritage List in July 2015.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Songs in history</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/songs/kesh-temple-hymn">Kesh Temple Hymn<span>c. 2600 BCE</span></a></p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Susa was settled by 4200 BCE in the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagros-mountains">Zagros</a> and became the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a>, then a capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> under Darius. Seleucid, Parthian, Sasanian and Arab rulers held it, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> razed it in 1218.</em></p>

